\par\par \b \uc2 Kenya shilling firms vs dlr, some banks cautious   \b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2007\par 13:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, May 22 (Reuters) - The Kenyan shilling <KES=> crept firmer against the dollar on Tuesday and some dealers said players were trading cautiously due to market concerns that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check further strengthening.   \par \par \uc2 At 1050 GMT, leading commercial banks traded the unit at 66.75/85 per dollar compared with 66.85/95 at the close of Friday's trade. It rose to 66.70/80 in earlier trade but demand by the telecommunications sector checked those gains.   \par \par \uc2 "People are looking at CBK ( \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to give any communication whether they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not," said Duncan Kimani, a dealer at  \uc2 Bank of Africa.\par \par \uc2 "People want to be on the safe side. No one wants to hold extreme positions."   \par \par \uc2 However, another trader said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to interfere with the market, although the local unit has strengthened to a four-year high.   \par \par \uc2 "I think CBK still believes the move is due to fundamentals," Peter Njuguna, a dealer at  \uc2 Commercial Bank of Africa\uc2 , said.   \par \par \uc2 Exporters have asked the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to check the rate as it was hurting their earnings.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it detected speculation. Traders said the next psychological barrier was the 66.50 level.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par          \par  <K