\par\par \b \uc2 East African  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  mixed vs dlr, Tanzanian up  \b0\par\par\uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2006\par 13:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, April 12 (Reuters) - East African \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded mixed against the U.S. dollar in the week to Wednesday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The Kenyan and Ugandan units were marginally weaker while the Tanzanian shilling strengthened on the back of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 KENYA\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2  shilling <KES=> was slightly weaker versus the dollar but traders expect it to stay in a range in coming days.  \par \par \uc2 At 1000 GMT, leading commercial banks posted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 71.05/71.25 compared with 71.00/71.20 at close of trading last Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The unit weakened to a low of 71.80/90 in early Tuesday's trade but clawed back its losses on some agricultural export supplies.  \par \par \uc2 "Demand and supply are kind of matching up," said Moses Kiboi, a senior dealer at Citibank, predicting a 71.00 - 71.80 range. "Unless we get any new factors, there is no reason for it to change out of its comfort zone."  \par \par \uc2 A Barclays Bank dealer said the local unit had weakened from dwindling inflows from tourism and horticulture industries.  \par \par \uc2 "The shilling has been weakening gradually because of importer demand and also we are seeing reduced flows from the tourism and horticulture sectors," the Barclays dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 UGANDA  \par \par \uc2 The Ugandan shilling <UGX=> eased against the greenback despite light corporate demand matching minimal U.S. dollar inflows, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Leading commercial banks quoted the local unit at 1,822/1,828 per dollar compared to last week's close of 1,825/1,830.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen some inflows from traditional sources like the non-governmental organisations and the tourism sector which have been matched by the light corporate demand," said Moses Elungat, head of trading at Barclays Bank.  \par \par \uc2 The shilling is expected to come under pressure as corporate players step up demand.  \par \par \uc2 "The shilling could remain under pressure as long as the current level of demand is sustained," another dealer said.  \par \par \uc2 "However, if the inflows outstrip dollar demand, then the shilling will ease off to levels of 1,825/1,830 after the Easter holidays this weekend."  \par \par \uc2 TANZANIA  \par \par \uc2 The Tanzania shilling <TZS=> appreciated slightly against the dollar on the back of dollar sales by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid weakening demand for greenbacks by oil importers, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Banks posted the unit at 1,222/1,227 shillings to a greenback compared with 1,225/1,230 at last Wednesday's close.  \par \par \uc2 "We saw Bank of Tanzania in the market selling dollars. This calmed the market a bit," said Alex Ngusaru, treasurer at Citibank. "They may sell more since they have enough foreign reserves. I don't see why they should not come in."  \par \par \uc2 Another dealer said the market received less dollar demand by oil importers as rains in the country have helped raise the water levels in hydroelectric dams.  \par \par \uc2 Tanzanian electricity authorities were earlier this year forced to cut supply by up to 12 hours per day due to a severe drought that had hit the region.  \par \par \uc2 Many people in the country were compelled to purchase fuel-run generators to provide power.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers anticipate more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should the shilling come under pressure, but they also expect banks to unwind long dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing some tightness in shilling liquidity and due to that, we might see banks offloading positions they hold which means an appreciation of the shilling in the short term," said Aziz Chacha, head of market making at Barclays Bank Tanzania.  \par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Sylvia Juuko in Kampala, Jack Kimball and Helen Nyambura-Mwaura in Nairobi)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-EAST AFRICA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|M|D|RBN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|AFN|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF|SXNA  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060415e24c0006q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
