\par\par \b \uc2 Strong Kenya  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  to slash tea farmers earnings  \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2006\par 15:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Feb 6 (Reuters) - Kenyan tea farmers could lose 10 percent of their earnings if the local shilling <KES=> keeps its strong tone against the dollar, a key agency grouping small-scale tea producers said on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The Kenyan shilling has been trading at a 2-1/2 year high against the dollar in the last few days and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBK) Governor Andrew Mullei has said he would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to influence the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Ours is an appeal to the government to do something about it because what will happen at the end of the year is that farmers will loose 10 percent of their income," David Kimani, the finance director at the Kenya Tea Development Agency (KTDA) told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "We are talking about a balance that ensures we exporters survive and importers too, survive."  \par \par \uc2 At 1400 GMT, the shilling traded at 71.00/20 shillings to a dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Kenya is one of the world's biggest exporters of black tea earning the east African country about 43 billion Kenya shillings ($604.8 million) annually.  \par \par \uc2 "The current exchange rates are literally killing the industry. I don't know how much longer we can operate at the shilling's current rate," Lerionka Tiampati, KTDA's managing director said.  \par \par \uc2 The agency, which accounts for over 60 percent of Kenya's annual tea production, said a severe drought in the country had affected production with some regions picking green leaf only three days in a week instead of six days.  \par \par \uc2 Industry players expect that poor rains in the last three years will slash the country's production by about 30 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Tea was once known as Kenya's green gold, but oversupply, spiralling production costs, stagnant prices, competition from emerging markets growing lower quality but cheaper tea and the trend towards coffee have hit the industry hard.  \par \par \uc2 KENYA-TEA-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|C|SOF|GRO|MTL|PCM  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060206e2260016s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
