\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-No let up in Kenya shilling rally despite concerns  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David Mageria  \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2005\par 12:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Sept 19 (Reuters) - The Kenya shilling is expected to continue its advance against the dollar in the short term and could touch the key 73/dollar level this week, but may struggle to break higher, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The shilling has gained nearly 6.8 percent so far this year, triggering complaints from local manufacturers and other exporters who say it has made their products uncompetitive.  \par \par \uc2 The shilling on Monday rose to a fresh five-and-a-half month high of 73.25/45.  \par \par \uc2 After pushing through a series of resistance levels in quick succession in the last few weeks, analysts say the 73.00 level is at hand.  \par \par \uc2 "All the flows that we have been seeing coming in from the export sector and the limited demand that we have been seeing from, especially the energy sector, have served to drive the shilling higher and higher," said Kihara Maina, the treasurer at Barclay Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "We are looking at touching the year high because the market is seeing it is within sight," he said, referring to the 73.10 level that was the highest the shilling has been in the past 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers said they expect the 73 level to provide strong resistance for the shilling.  \par \par \uc2 "We may see some importers taking advantage of these low levels to take up forwards and cover their requirements through to the end of the year," said Timothy Kabiru, treasurer at the Cooperative Bank of Kenya.  \par \par \uc2 CURRENT ACCOUNT  \par \par \uc2 Kenya's current account deficit widened by $838 million in the 12 months to May 2005, in part due to high global oil prices and increased imports of machinery and transport equipment, suggesting the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have come under pressure.  \par \par \uc2 However, heavy inflows particularly from tea, horticulture and coffee exporters, combined with receipts from a booming tourism sector, have lent strong support for the shilling.  \par \par \uc2 Even more significant are relatively high domestic interest rates. The average yield on benchmark 91-day treasury bill stood at 8.5 percent at the last auction on Thursday compared with 2.9 percent posted the same time last year.  \par \par \uc2 "Obviously people are keen to get the shilling yield at rates of about eight and a half percent as opposed to the dollar at about three and a half percent," Barclay's Maina said.  \par \par \uc2 The high rates also have discouraged importers, especially oil companies, from hedging themselves in the forward market.  \par \par \uc2 While the shilling's rally has led many importers to stay on the sidelines, a recent comment by \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Andrew Mullei that the gains reflected the country's improved economic performance was providing further support.  \par \par \uc2 "Comments like the one that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has issued that this is a reflection of the economy and things like that, that has given an impetus to the shilling," said Jeremiah Kendagor, the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2  Commercial Bank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Mullei, who has rejected calls to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to weaken the shilling, said 10 days ago that the exchange rate reflected the brisk growth in the domestic economy and investor optimism which was attracting strong foreign inflows.  \par \par \uc2 The Kenyan economy expanded by 4.3 percent in 2004 compared with 2.8 percent in 2003. A survey by a leading local research firm on business confidence released last week showed that most chief executives were bullish on the economy's prospects.  \par \par \uc2 "What you see now with the shilling is a by-product of a very rapidly growing economy. Because if the economy was declining in performance, we would be seeing the exchange rate depreciating," Mullei told reporters at a function where he released the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy on Sept. 9.  \par \par \uc2 The governor said he expected to see the shilling weaken as demand for imports to support the growing econonomy increases.  \par \par \uc2 "I see the reversal of the exchange rate of the shilling torwards either stability or depreciation."  \par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by George Obulutsa)  \par \par \uc2 KENYA-SHILLING (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|AFN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020050919e19j000o1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
