\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Kenya ready to end shilling speculation-c.bank.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Wallis   \par \uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2004\par 17:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NAIROBI, Aug 27 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to stem a slide in the shilling, remains ready to ensure an end to speculation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and some risk takers "stand to lose", the bank's governor said on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Andrew Mullei told a news conference that a fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  below 79 to the U.S. dollar - the shilling actually hit a historic low of 83.25/45 on Wednesday - could not be explained by fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stands ready to make sure the speculative activity is brought to an end. Some risk takers stand to lose," Mullei said, speaking after the market closed.   \par \par \uc2 "We are moving very cautiously because it is not the intention of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control the level of the    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  exchange rate....The purpose of us going to the market is to\par  protect the interests of importers."\par    The bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday to prop up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 which has fallen steadily from around 79.60 three months ago. It was unchanged in early dealings at 81.00/20 on Friday, later firming to 80.86/81.06.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The slide has been blamed on several factors including hints by donors that they might pull aid out of Kenya over graft allegations and high global oil prices.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The exchange rate beyond 79 cannot be explained by fundamentals," Mullei said. "If it were an excess of demand compared to supply (of dollars) we would not have proceeded to the market to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It is not our intention to cause losses for any players in the market. But if we warn some players are overplaying, but you don't see a reaction then you have to become mechanical (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DONOR WARNINGS   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The shilling began coming under heavy pressure in July when the European Union (EU) said it had postponed a decision on an aid package Kenya needs to finance its budget.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The EU deferred consideration of 125 million euros in budget support until September or October, although it said longer-term development aid would continue.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move was a fresh sign of donor impatience with the government's faltering anti-corruption campaign and fuelled fears that donors were considering suspending aid the east African country desperately needs to escape from poverty.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The EU and other top international donors warned Kenya earlier in July that it risked losing vital funding, saying corruption still appeared to be at the heart of government despite President Mwai Kibaki's promises to stamp it out.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kenya's 2004/05 (July/June) budget depends heavily on aid. Presenting the budget last month, Finance Minister David Mwiraria said 50.7 billion shillings ($633 million), or 11.5 percent of total spending, would be donor-funded.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Mullei said even if donors withheld promised funds, the government had enough in reserve to maintain a stable shilling.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Even if our development partners delay until the end of the year, the effect of that delay would not affect the reserves needed to keep the exchange rate stable," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said key indicators over the last two years, particularly inflation, all showed Kenya's economy was on the right track.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "To keep inflation below three percent, for a developing country running a budget deficit, is really a success. Kenya's performance on that score is exemplary," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is steady progress for the Kenyan economy. The thing is working, and working in the way that it should be working."    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040827e08r00a76\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
