\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Kenya cenbank sees stable shilling ahead of poll. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wangui Kanina \par \uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Oktober 2002\par 12:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  DAR ES SALAAM, Oct 25 (Reuters) - Kenya's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said on Friday the shilling would end the year below a key psychological level despite pressure from impending elections. \par \par \uc2  Nahashon Nyagah told Reuters in Tanzania's capital Dar Es Salaam that the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would range-trade from 78 to 79 shillings per dollar, just under the key psychological level of 80 shillings despite steady weakening since August. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think it will hit the 80 (shillings) mark against the dollar," Nyagah said. \par \par \uc2  Kenya is expected to hold general elections in December when veteran President Daniel arap Moi is due to step down after 24 years at the helm and the local unit has weakened as the market reacts to the uncertainty of change. \par \par \uc2  But Nyagah said there were no economic fundamentals to indicate the shilling should weaken further and said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt any swings in the exchange rate that are not supported by fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "In the past one and a half years we have not had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a single day, we do not want to," Nyagah said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not usually reveal when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market but has repeatedly warned banks it could use its relatively strong $1.1 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to influence the rate. \par \par \uc2  Nyagah also moved to reassure investors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is independent of the government and would not suffer any attempts to fiddle with monetary policy for electoral reasons. \par \par \uc2  He said legislation barred the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from printing money to fund the forthcoming elections, as happened in the run-up to the 1992 elections when Moi's government printed money to finance the ruling party's campaign, creating a deep post-election economic slump. \par \par \uc2  "It is not a request I have received from the government, it will not happen and it will never happen as long as I am governor," Nyagah said. \par \par \uc2  He said lack of international donor support was partly to blame for Kenya's sputtering economy, which grew at a modest 1.2 percent in 2001 compared with 5.6 percent in Tanzania. But he added he expected lending to Kenya to resume early next year when a new parliament is constituted. \par \par \uc2  Officials hope the new parliament will conclude the passage of anti-corruption bills demanded by the International Monetary Fund, whose blessing is a key criteria for many donors. \par \par \uc2  The Corruption Control Bill 2002, which sets up an independent anti-corruption body and the Public Officers' Ethics Bill which demands that all senior public servants including the president declare their wealth, have been pending in parliament despite passing several crucial stages of debate. \par \par \uc2  "The bills are ready to go to parliament, and that is all we have left and once parliament is back I see that we'll be able to get back on the IMF programme so I see the economy getting much better," Nyagah said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts were not in total agreement with Nyagah. \par \par \uc2  Some say the shilling will breach 80 shillings by the end of the year. But most agree it will not weaken much further. \par \par \uc2  Leading commercial banks posted the shilling at a mean rate of 79.55/65 to the dollar at 0800 GMT from 79.25/50 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was edgy and attributed the shilling's falls to interbank speculation as banks move to stock up on dollars ahead of the weekend. \par \par \uc2  "The shilling is expected to remain under pressure as dealers square positions ahead of the weekend," Citibank said in a Friday market report. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021025dyap00kdn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
