\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Kenya \highlight\uc2  shilling gains on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk. \b0\par\par\uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2002\par 14:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NAIROBI, June 14 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2  shilling bounced from near 18-month lows on Friday, gaining up to 70 cents against the dollar as traders reacted to a report that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it had the resources to support the local unit. \par \par \uc2  The shilling hit a peak of 78.60/70 to the dollar in early trade compared to 79.30/40 at Thursday's close. At 1220 GMT the shilling eased slightly to 78.80/90 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The local unit had depreciated sharply as importers bought dollars as a precaution ahead of the budget. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Nahashon Nyaga was quoted by the East African Standard daily as saying he had adequate resources to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and correct the shilling's depreciation. \par \par \uc2  There was no immediate comment from Nyagah. \par \par \uc2  Anthony Wachira of Kenya Commercial Bank's treasury department said the shilling had gained on the report. \par \par \uc2  "To a large extent the shilling's gain was prompted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Wachira. \par \par \uc2  "There is a likelihood it will strengthen even further because the clear signal from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is that the shilling should be stronger at levels of about 78.30/40." \par \par \uc2  Kenya's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have risen steadily to $1.077 billion by March 2002 or 3.4 months of import cover. \par \par \uc2  But other dealers attributed the gains to a correction after the budget announcement on Thursday by Finance Minister Chris Obure for 2002/03 came in at the market's expectations. \par \par \uc2  Traders said a rise in Kenya's budget deficit to four percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in 2001/02 (July-June) from a 1.8 percent deficit the previous year was slightly better than market expectations. \par \par \uc2  "People overspeculated on the budget - since it was going up on hot air it had to go down on hot air," said Maina Kihara at Barclays Bank. \par \par \uc2  The shilling touched 79.45/50 early in Thursday's session, its lowest level for 18 months, as fears over the size of the gap in government finances weighed. The local unit last hit 79.50 to the dollar on December 7, 2000. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market was driving up the dollar against the shilling on the premise that there would be a huge budget deficit that would therefore impact on how the shilling should behave," Kihara said. \par \par \uc2  "But yesterday there was really nothing new. Everyone knew there was going to be a budget deficit." \par \par \uc2  The widened budget deficit reflected increasing spending ahead of elections due this year and a funding gap left by the suspension of donor lending in 2000 over corruption concerns. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the size of the deficit was roughly in line with expectations. \par \par \uc2  "The only thing I can put it down to is that this is a speculator's market," said Fred Michuki at Standard Chartered Bank, adding that all the activity in Friday's busy market was down to interbank players. \par \par \uc2  "There has been a lot of talk pre-budget, as in the last few years, and the shilling tends to weaken before a budget and strengthen after it. There are no fundamentals." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020614dy6e00o2t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
