\par\par \b \uc2  Kenya shilling seen on defensive ahead of budget. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Manoah Esipisu \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 1999\par 11:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NAIROBI, May 31 (Reuters) - The Kenyan shilling will remain on the defensive ahead of the 1999/2000 state budget on June 10, undermined by higher importer demand and shortfalls in agriculture exports, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  They said they saw the shilling in the 69 to 71 range against the dollar, with any breach of 72 expected to trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  The shilling opened lower again on Monday with commercial banks quoting it at 70.80/71.05 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The shilling remains vulnerable to reduced dollar sales by exporters, believed to be withholding funds from the market in the hope of benefiting from further shilling softness," said Razia Khan, of the Standard Chartered Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The recent experience of rapid weakening will likely see sustained importer demand for forward cover, reinforcing the shilling's downward bias," added Khan, a treasury economist. \par \par \uc2  Kenya's financial markets and foreign donors hope the government will present a budget that includes concrete measures to cut government spending, rebuild the crumbling infrastructure and improve its supervision of commercial banks. \par \par \uc2  A good budget would likely boost the shilling even though the export sector remains weak. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the shilling has been hurt by developments in the tea and coffee sectors - Kenya's main commodity exports. \par \par \uc2  They said low world coffee prices and a dispute on marketing rules among smallholder coffee societies were likely to offset the advantages of increased production. \par \par \uc2  Smallholders produce about half Kenya's total coffee crop. \par \par \uc2  The outlook for tea, the top hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earner, was also gloomy. Despite a forecast recovery from below-average rains earlier this year, Kenya could face the loss of an important export market. \par \par \uc2  Retaliatory measures in response to tariffs imposed on Pakistani rice by Kenya have led to lobbying by the Pakistani Chamber of Commerce to impose similar tariffs on Kenyan tea. \par \par \uc2  The Pakistani decision has not been finalised and could contravene World Trade Organisation (WTO) rules. But the last two months have witnessed a fall in Pakistani purchases at Kenyan tea auctions, depressing competition and keeping prices low. \par \par \uc2  Markets are also awaiting the resumption of International Monetary Fund (IMF) lending to Kenya, on which hopes have long been placed to boost investor confidence and domestic growth. \par \par \uc2  The IMF board of directors met Kenyan Treasury officials this month in Washington but failed to set a date for the resumption of talks on the stalled three-year $205 million loan under its Enhanced Structural Adjustment facility (ESAF). \par \par \uc2  "The shilling will continue its downward trajectory in the absence of positive news from that front," said a senior dealer at a bank in Nairobi. "That news should be available in July." \par \par \uc2  Hints by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Kenya\b0 \highlight\uc2  (CBK) that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be forthcoming if the shilling reached an unacceptable level, thought to be around 70 versus the dollar, have helped to shore up support for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will only smooth out fluctuations in the exchange rate not caused by economic fundamentals, rather than attempt to set a direction for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has blamed shilling losses mainly on a rapid decline in interest rates. The yield on the benchmark 91-day treasury bills now stands at below 10 percent from a peak of 27 percent in April last year. \par \par \uc2  CBK Chief Dealer Charles Musau told reporters this month that low interest rates meant offshore dollars were now chasing markets offering better yields - and that put constant pressure on the shilling. \par \par \uc2  ((Nairobi newsroom ++254-2 330261, fax 254-2 338860, nairobi.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv5v07e12\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
